{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Wave Review (Multi-Agent)",
  "description": "Independent adversarial review of the current changes by four parallel reviewers: domain fidelity against the IFC, non-adjudication under ADR-0003, data integrity, and a design-level semantic review. Run before closing a wave.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Run the wave review protocol on the current changes.\n\nThis project's planning documents were independently reviewed in August 2026 and the review found real errors that self-review had missed — wrong counts, a missing state, a bug in an ADR that would have caused the exact harm another ADR exists to prevent. `docs/audit/validation-findings-2026-08.md` is the record. That is what this protocol is for. Do not review your own work in a single pass; fan out.\n\n## Step 1 — Establish the diff\n\nRun `git --no-pager diff HEAD` and `git --no-pager status --short`. Always use `--no-pager`; the pager blocks execution. If the working tree is clean, use `git --no-pager diff HEAD~1` and say so. Identify which wave this is from `docs/hr1-readiness/README.md` and read that wave's file for its acceptance criteria.\n\n## Step 2 — Fan out, in parallel\n\nInvoke all four of the following with `invoke_sub_agent` **in a single message so they run in parallel**. Do not run them sequentially and do not do the review yourself instead — independence is the entire point.\n\nGive each reviewer the concrete diff or the specific file paths and line ranges it needs. A sub-agent that has to go find the change will review the wrong thing.\n\n**Reviewer 1 — Domain fidelity.** Use `context-gatherer`. Ask: does every domain claim in this diff match `docs/domain/cms-2454-ifc/rule-extract.md` and `docs/domain/supporting-regs/`? Every threshold, lookback, conversion, deadline, and exception condition must carry a CFR citation, and the citation must actually say what the code assumes. Flag any claim resting on the IFC's characterization of another regulation rather than that regulation's text. Flag any policy value hardcoded outside `src/lib/policy/`. Report the citation you verified against, not a summary.\n\n**Reviewer 2 — Non-adjudication.** Use `general-task-execution`. Ask: does anything in this diff assert a determination HourKeep is not allowed to make? Check ADR-0003 and `.kiro/steering/compliance-copy-standards.md`. Look at four layers, not just copy: (a) user-facing strings — \"you are exempt\", \"compliant\", \"you automatically meet\"; (b) type and field names that encode a verdict, such as `isCompliant` or `complianceStatus`; (c) return values that collapse facts into a pass/fail; (d) **behavior** — hiding or disabling a feature because the user looks excluded is a stronger assertion than a sentence, and needs a visible way back to screening. Also confirm each user-facing number is labelled Computed, Conditional, or Deferred, and that every hedge is paired with a concrete next action.\n\n**Reviewer 3 — Data integrity.** Use `general-task-execution`. Ask: can this diff lose or corrupt a user's data? There is no server and no backup; records live in one browser's IndexedDB. Check `.kiro/steering/data-migration-standards.md`. Specifically: does any Dexie `.upgrade()` drop, rename in place, or fail to backfill? Does the version number collide with another wave's claim? Is there a migration test with a realistic fixture from the previous version? Do deletes cascade to documents and blobs, or silently orphan them? Does any reader of a changed field remain unupdated — grep the field name across `src/`, don't trust the type checker. Are month boundaries handled with `date-fns` rather than raw `Date` arithmetic?\n\n**Reviewer 4 — Semantic review.** Use `semantic_reviewer` on the local diff for a design-level read organized by concern rather than by file.\n\n## Step 3 — Verify before you report\n\nEvery sub-agent finding is a **lead, not a finding**. Before it enters your report, open the file and confirm it. Sub-agents hallucinate line numbers and occasionally invent the problem. Drop anything you could not confirm, and say how many you dropped.\n\nThen run the mechanical checks yourself: `npx tsc --noEmit`, `npm run lint`, `npm run format:check`, `npm run build`, and `npm test` if a test script exists. Do not start a dev server — it blocks, and nothing here needs it.\n\n## Step 4 — Report\n\n- **Verdict on each acceptance criterion** for this wave: met, not met, or unverifiable — and for each, the observable you checked. A criterion you can't check against something runnable, readable, or visible is a criterion that needs rewriting; say so.\n- **Findings by severity.** Blocking (data loss, a false statement about the law, an asserted determination) / should-fix / note. Each with file, line, and the citation or rule it violates.\n- **Negative-criterion check.** Did any criterion get satisfied by deletion? Removing a wrong section also removes whatever was true in it. Name what was lost.\n- **What you could not verify**, explicitly. An unverified claim flagged as unverified costs nothing; unflagged it costs a rewrite.\n- **Counts you actually computed.** If you didn't compute it, write \"roughly\" or leave it out.\n\nDo not fix anything during the review. Report, then ask what to fix."
  }
}
